WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.077
CASE branch order becomes critical when conditions overlap.

00:00:04.077 --> 00:00:09.892
Suppose we want to label people as Senior when age is at least sixty-five and Adult when age

00:00:09.892 --> 00:00:15.962
is at least eighteen. If we check age greater than or equal to eighteen first,

00:00:15.962 --> 00:00:19.512
a seventy-year-old patient matches that branch immediately.

00:00:19.512 --> 00:00:22.067
CASE stops and returns Adult.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.797
The Senior branch is never reached.

00:00:24.797 --> 00:00:31.392
The correct order is to place the more specific or higher threshold first: check for Senior, then Adult.

00:00:31.392 --> 00:00:35.752
This same mistake appears in many real systems.

00:00:35.752 --> 00:00:41.472
A fraud score above ninety might be Critical, while a score above fifty is Review.

00:00:41.472 --> 00:00:46.387
If the Review condition comes first, the Critical category disappears.

00:00:46.387 --> 00:00:52.102
A bill above ten thousand may be High-Value, while above five thousand is Standard.

00:00:52.102 --> 00:00:54.582
High-Value needs to come first.

00:00:54.582 --> 00:00:59.387
A useful test is to ask whether one condition logically contains another.

00:00:59.387 --> 00:01:04.142
Every value above ten thousand is also above five thousand.

00:01:04.142 --> 00:01:07.462
Every senior adult is also an adult.

00:01:07.462 --> 00:01:13.062
When one condition contains another, the narrower condition usually belongs first.

00:01:13.062 --> 00:01:16.392
Run both versions and compare older patients.

00:01:16.392 --> 00:01:19.342
You are not just learning CASE syntax here.

00:01:19.342 --> 00:01:22.084
You are learning how order can encode priority.
